{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 560,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine for a second that you are invited to a \ngame of chance. There is a room with 100 people.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are told that the average net worth \nin this room is $2 billion. Naturally,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you think your odds are fantastic. You pick a \nperson at random, hoping to trade bank accounts.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21720,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But here is the catch. 99 of those people \nhave exactly $100,000 in their pockets.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The hundth person is Elon Musk. In this scenario, \nthe average is technically correct, but the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36680,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reality for 99% of the people in that room is \nthat they are nowhere near that $2 billion mark.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is exactly what is happening every single \ntime you try to pick an individual stock.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50080,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are looking at the historical 10% return of \nthe stock market and assuming you can just grab  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55600,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a piece of it by picking a few good companies. \nBut the math of the stock market is not a normal  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62000,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distribution. It is not a fair coin flip. It \nis a game of extreme statistical asymmetry  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69480,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where a microscopic number of winners \ncarry the entire team on their backs. If  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 74920,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you miss those specific winners, you do not just \nunderperform slightly, you fail catastrophically.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81880,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we are going to look past the Wall Street \nmarketing and the flashy earnings reports. We are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88320,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "going to dive into the cold hard realm of standard \ndeviation and positive skewess to see why even the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94800,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smartest people on the planet are mathematically \ndestined to lose to a simple unmanaged index fund.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102360,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you value your retirement, this is the \nmost important math lesson you will ever take.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108720,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get into the heavy math, I want to make \na quick observation about the world we live in.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 114720,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are currently surrounded by what I call \nthe financial entertainment complex. There  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 120880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are thousands of hours of television, millions \nof articles, and an endless stream of social  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 126640,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "media gurus telling you that you can find the \nnext Apple, the next Amazon, or the next Tesla.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 134080,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is an intoxicating narrative because it \nsuggests that through enough research and grit,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139320,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you can outsmart the system. I have spent years \nlooking at these portfolios and I have noticed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something consistent. The effort you put in almost \nnever correlates to the reward you get back.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152520,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In most areas of life, if you work 10 \ntimes harder, you get a better result.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157880,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In investing, the harder you try to beat the \nmarket, the more likely you are to fall behind it.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 164000,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This video is going to be a deep \ndive into why that paradox exists.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you find this kind of datadriven approach \nvaluable, the best return on your time today  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173400,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is to stick around until we reach the \nBessembbinder study in a few minutes.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177720,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It fundamentally changed how I view my own \nportfolio, and it will likely do the same for you.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183720,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are going to look at why the pros fail, how \nstandard deviation acts as a silent killer,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189400,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and why the most boring way to invest is \nactually the most elite strategy on the planet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195080,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let us start with a reality check on \nthe people who do this for a living.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199400,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If stockpicking were truly a skill that could \nbe mastered with enough education and resources,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204400,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we would expect the professionals to be crushing \nthe market. These are people with Ivy League MBAs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210520,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Bloomberg terminals that cost $30,000 a \nyear, and direct access to corporate CEOs.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217040,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet, when we look at the SPA scorecard, which is \nthe gold standard for measuring active management,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 222640,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the data is a total graveyard. According to \nthe year-end reports, over a 15-year period,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229120,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nearly 88% of all large cap active fund managers \nin the United States failed to beat the S\u0026P 500.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236880,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you stretch that out to 20 years, \nthat number often climbs above 90%.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think about that for a second. If you went \nto a doctor and they told you that 90% of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246960,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the people in their profession failed to achieve \na basic standard result, you would never go back.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253200,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in finance, we keep giving these people our \nmoney. Now, the common excuse is that fees are the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259360,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "problem. If a manager charges 1% and the market \nreturns 10%, they have to hit 11 just to tie.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267280,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is true and that variance drag is a real \nproblem. But even before you account for fees,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 273160,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gross returns of these \nprofessionals still trail the index.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277440,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This suggests that the failure is not \njust about the cost of doing business.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It is a structural failure in the act of stock \nselection itself. The professionals are not losing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because they are lazy or unintelligent. They are \nlosing because they are fighting a war against  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291920,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mathematical structure of the market. \nThey are looking for needles in a haystack.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296960,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And most of the time they are not only missing \nthe needle, they are getting poked by the straw.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303040,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It actually gets worse when you account \nfor what we call survivorship bias.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308160,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you look at a list of mutual funds today, \nyou are only seeing the winners who lived long  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "enough to tell the tale. A massive percentage of \nfunds that exist at the start of a 10-year period  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318880,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "simply vanish by the end of it. They are \nliquidated or merged into other funds  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324440,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because their performance was so abysmal that \nthe parent company wanted to hide the evidence.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330080,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you adjust the numbers to include \nall the funds that died along the way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333880,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the success rate of picking stocks \nbecomes a statistical rounding error.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339000,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I have run these numbers myself and it is shocking \nhow much marketing goes into hiding the cemetery.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344960,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We see the one fund out of 50 that beat the market \nand we think, \"Wow, they have the secret source.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351720,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We do not see the 49 others that are currently 6 \nft under.\" This creates an illusion of competence.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358360,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It makes us think that because someone won, it was \ndue to skill rather than the law of large numbers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364840,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But why does this happen? Why is it \nso hard to just pick the winners?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 369880,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand that we have to move away from the \ncharts and move into the world of statistics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375560,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to talk about the bell curve and why it is \na complete lie when it comes to your money. Most  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381480,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of us are wired to expect a normal distribution \nwhere things cluster around an average.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387080,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We think if we buy 30 stocks, some will do well, \nsome will do poorly, and most will be okay.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393520,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the stock market does not follow a bell \ncurve. It follows a logn normal positively skewed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399000,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distribution. The human brain loves a good bell \ncurve. If you measure the height of 10,000 men,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406680,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "most will be around 5'9. You will have a \nfew giants and a few very short people,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412600,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the average and the median \nwill be basically the same.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because we see this in nature, we \ninstinctively apply it to our portfolios.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422040,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We assume that if the market's average return is \n10%. Most stocks will return somewhere near 10%.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429480,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This assumption is the foundation of \nthe stockpicking delusion. In reality,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434640,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "individual stock returns are wild and asymmetric. \nWhen we talk about standard deviation in finance,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441800,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we are measuring how much an investment's \nreturns disperse from its average.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446680,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A high standard deviation means things are all \nover the place. For the S\u0026P 500 as a whole,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453560,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the annualized standard deviation is usually \naround 15 to 18%. That is manageable.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461080,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the standard deviation of an individual \nstock, it is often 40, 50, or even 100%.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469120,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Individual companies are chaotic. They deal with \nscandals, failed products, and new competitors.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476600,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you own the whole index, these \nindividual disasters cancel each other out.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482040,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But when you try to pick 30 winners, \nyou are exposing yourself to the full  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486240,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unadulterated variance of individual equities. \nAnd because of positive skewess, that variance  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492920,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is not working in your favor. In a normal \ndistribution, the middle point is the average.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499640,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the stock market, the massive winners on \nthe far right side pull the average way up,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504800,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the median stock, the one right in \nthe middle, actually returns almost nothing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 510680,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Elon Musk in the room \nproblem, but with ticker symbols.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515640,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let us break down why this positive skewess is a \nmathematical death sentence for the stock picker.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think about the potential of a single stock. \nWhat is the worst that can happen? You lose 100%.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529520,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That is your flaw. You cannot lose more than you \nput in. But what is the best that can happen?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536040,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The upside is theoretically infinite. A \nstock like Amazon or Apple has grown by  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tens of thousands of percentage points over the \ndecades. Because the downside is capped at 100,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 547480,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the upside has no ceiling. The return \ndistribution gets stretched far out to the right.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553120,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is what we call a long right tail. When \nyou have a few outliers that return 5,000%,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they pull the average of the whole market up to \n10%. But most of the stocks in that market are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564840,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not returning 10%. They are returning zero or they \nare losing money. I want you to let that sink in.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572760,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you pick a stock at random, the highest \nprobability outcome over a long period is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 577400,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you will make no money at all. You are essentially \nbetting that you can find the one in a thousand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582360,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "outlier that is doing all the heavy lifting. If \nyou miss that specific stock, your portfolio's  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 588160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average will not be the market average. It will be \nmuch, much lower. This is why the pros struggle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They build a diversified portfolio of 50 stocks, \nbut if they decide that the one stock that goes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up 2,000% is overvalued and they leave it out, \ntheir entire fund is doomed to underperform.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607520,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are playing a game where missing one single \nanswer out of a 100 results in a failing grade.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613360,
    "dDurationMs": 2960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We are going to look at the study \nthat proved this beyond any doubt.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you are still thinking that you can beat the \nodds, I need to tell you about Hrik Bessenbinder.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624040,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He is a finance professor who published a paper \nthat basically set the academic world on fire.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629840,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He analyzed the lifetime returns of every single \ncommon stock in the United States from 1926 all  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636600,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the way to 2016. That is over 25,000 companies. \nHis findings were the ultimate reality check.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645120,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First, he found that over half of all \nstocks had a negative lifetime return.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650000,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most common outcome for an individual \nstock was a total loss of 100%. Second,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656160,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he discovered that 96% of all stocks collectively \nperformed no better than a one-mon treasury bill.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663000,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means you could have taken the money \nfrom nearly every public company in history,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667160,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "put it in a boring government cash account, and \nended up with the same amount of wealth. Here is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672800,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the kicker. All of the net wealth created by the \nstock market over those 90 years, $35 trillion,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679840,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was generated by just 4% of the companies. Just \nover 1,000 companies created all the value.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687160,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other 24,000 were basically just noise. \nThis is the 4% rule of the stock market.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693680,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you own the index, you are guaranteed \nto own that 4%. If you pick stocks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698840,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have a 96% chance of picking the companies \nthat perform no better than a savings account.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704560,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When people talk about beating the market, they \nare talking about finding that tiny 4% before  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709600,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "everyone else does. But when you consider that \neven the brightest minds on Wall Street miss them,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715360,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have to ask yourself, why am I taking this \nrisk? We are going to talk about the agony and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721520,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ecstasy of these positions next. To add some \nmodern flavor to Bessenbinder's historical data,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728840,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we can look at a report from JP Morgan \ncalled the agony and the ecstasy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734800,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They looked at the Russell \n3000 index from 1980 to 2020.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739520,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They found that 40% of all stocks in that index \nsuffered what they called a catastrophic loss.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 746360,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is defined as a stock dropping 70% or \nmore from its peak and never recovering.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Imagine 40% of your choices just evaporating \nand never coming back. Meanwhile,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758880,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the overall index was up massively during that \nsame period. Why? Because the ecstasy stocks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765680,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the top 10%, performed so well that they made \nup for all the bankruptcies and all the lagards.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 772640,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is where market cap waiting becomes your \nbest friend. An index fund like the S\u0026P 500 is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779080,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a ruthless, emotionless momentum machine. When a \ncompany starts to fail and its market cap shrinks,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786240,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the index automatically reduces its exposure \nto that company. If a company goes to zero,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792240,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the index only loses the tiny fraction \nof a percent that it represented.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797520,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But when a company like Nvidia or Apple starts to \nexplode, the index automatically buys more of it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803400,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as it grows. The S\u0026P 500 lets its winners run to \ninfinity and it cuts its losers as they shrink.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Most human investors do the exact opposite. We \nhave a psychological urge to sell our winners  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817920,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to lock in profits and hold on to our losers \nbecause we don't want to realize a loss.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823600,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This behavioral flaw is a direct attack on \nthe math of positive skewess. To win the game,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829880,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you have to hold the outliers forever. The index \ndoes this perfectly. You and I, unfortunately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836720,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are human, which makes us very bad at it. I \nhope this deep dive into the math is helping  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842760,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you see the market through a clearer lens. If \nyou appreciate this kind of unscentimental,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848480,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "datadriven approach to money, I want to invite \nyou to become a bigger part of this community.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854360,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can click the join button below to become \none of the optimizers. Our members get a few cool  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860360,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perks like custom sketch badges next to their \nnames and priority replies to their comments.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866680,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I try to get to as many questions as I can, but I \nalways start with the optimizers because they help  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "keep this channel independent and focused on the \nfacts rather than the hype. It is a simple way to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 877880,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "support the research that goes into these long- \nform breakdowns. But regardless of whether you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 883280,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "join, I am glad you are here. We still have the \nmost famous bet in financial history to discuss  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889680,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which proves that even billionaires know \nthey cannot beat the index consistently.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 894720,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let us look at why Warren Buffett bet a million \ndollars on everything we have just talked about.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 900680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Warren Buffett is often cited as the ultimate \nproof that stockpicking works. And yes, he is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a phenomenal outlier. But Buffett himself would \ntell you that he is a rare statistical event.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913280,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 2008, he decided to prove the \nmath of indexing to the world.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918120,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He bet a million dollars that a simple, lowcost \nS\u0026P 500 index fund would outperform a hand-picked  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925720,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "basket of elite hedge funds over a 10-year period. \nA group called Protegé Partners took the bet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 933880,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They picked five funds of funds which represented \nthe expertise of hundreds of the smartest hedge  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 939720,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fund managers on the planet. These were \npeople using leverage, complex derivatives,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945680,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and highfrequency data to gain an edge. By the end \nof the 10 years, the result was a total blowout.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953480,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The S\u0026P 500 fund returned an annualized \n8.5%. The elite hedge fund managers,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they averaged only 2.9%. Buffett won \nthe bet without breaking a sweat.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967920,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He did not win because he was smarter than the \nhedge fund managers. He won because he sat still.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 974640,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He understood that the index would naturally \ncapture the massive right tail winners while the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979920,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hedge fund managers were busy trading in and out \nof stocks, paying high fees, and missing the few  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985560,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companies that actually mattered. This bet should \nbe the final nail in the coffin for the idea that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 991720,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "active management is a superior path. If the \nsmartest guys in the room with all the money in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 997600,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world cannot beat a dumb index over a decade, \nwhy do we think we can do it on our lunch breaks?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1004840,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The answer lies in our psychology, which we \nneed to fix if we want to actually build wealth.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1010840,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We have to learn to accept average as an elite \nresult. The reason we ignore the math is usually  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1017160,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to three big psychological traps. The first \nis overconfidence. We all think we are above  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1024780,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average drivers and we all think we have a special \nknack for seeing which companies will succeed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1031280,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The second is survivorship bias from the \nmedia. You never see a headline that says,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1036920,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"Man loses everything by investing in a \nboring industrial company that went bankrupt.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1042600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You only see the stories about the 25year-old \nwho turned $1,000 into a million with Nvidia.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1049280,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This gives us the illusion that the outliers are \ncommon. The third is the illusion of control.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1056360,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We feel like we are doing something productive \nwhen we research a stock. Buying an index fund  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1061920,
    "dDurationMs": 7720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feels passive and lazy. But in the stock market, \nlaziness is a superpower. Every time you trade,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1069640,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are risking the chance of selling one of that \n4% of stocks that will carry your retirement.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1075560,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The most important realization you can \nhave is that in the world of investing,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1079520,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "average is actually exceptional. Because of the \nway the math works, if you simply match the market  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1085840,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "return by owning an index, you will outperform \nabout 90% of professionals over 20 years. You are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1092840,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not settling for a C-grade. You are getting \nan A+ just by showing up and doing nothing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1098920,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the ultimate emancipation for the \nretail investor. You no longer need to stress  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1104160,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over earnings calls or watch financial news. You \ncan acknowledge the math of standard deviation,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1110320,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "buy the entire haystack, and let the \nmassive outliers do the work for you.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115400,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You are no longer a gambler. You are a collector \nof the world's most productive assets. That  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1121240,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "shift in perspective is the difference between a \nlife of anxiety and a life of financial freedom.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1128800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we look at the math, the stockpicking \ndelusion falls apart. Standard deviation shows  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1134160,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us the risk of concentration. Bessenbinder shows \nus that wealth is concentrated in a tiny fraction  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1139840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of companies and the SPA data shows us that even \nthe pros cannot find those companies consistently.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146400,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The solution is to stop looking for the needle and \njust buy the haystack. It is simple. It is boring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1152760,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it is mathematically the most sound \nway to grow your wealth over time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1157680,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I want to hear your thoughts on this. Have \nyou ever had a stock that you thought was a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1161280,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "sure winner only to see it become part of that 40% \ncatastrophic loss category? Or are you a diehard  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1168440,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "indexer who has learned to love the average? \nDrop a comment below and let us do some peer  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1173160,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "review on these strategies. I read as many as I \ncan, especially from our optimizers. Thank you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1178920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for spending your time with me today. Stay smart, \nstay patient, and I will see you in the next one."
    } ]
  } ]
}
